487
00:26:32,720 --> 00:26:35,260
So now let's shift
the conversation.

488
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:37,460
Want to talk
electrolyte services?

489
00:26:37,660 --> 00:26:38,540
Okay.

490
00:26:39,340 --> 00:26:42,120
As you can tell, we're
going to first start by

491
00:26:42,120 --> 00:26:45,200
talking about potassium.
But before we do that, when

492
00:26:45,200 --> 00:26:48,580
it comes to electrolytes,
we can have a lot of

493
00:26:48,580 --> 00:26:53,160
electrolytes or a low
concentration of electrolytes.

494
00:26:53,760 --> 00:26:56,840
Now I want to clue you guys
into a general paradigm

495
00:26:56,840 --> 00:27:01,800
that we can use that works
about 90% of the time when it

496
00:27:01,800 --> 00:27:04,920
comes to electrolyte
concentrations and ECG changes.

497
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:09,540
About 97% of the time,
again, this works. So

498
00:27:09,540 --> 00:27:14,280
when we have a high
electrolyte concentration,

499
00:27:14,280 --> 00:27:19,160
like in hyperkalemia
or hypercalcemia, two

500
00:27:19,160 --> 00:27:22,400
things that we can note
and look for are QT and

501
00:27:22,400 --> 00:27:26,540
QRS changes. Specifically,
we will get widening

502
00:27:26,540 --> 00:27:32,560
of the QRS complex and
a short QT interval.

503
00:27:33,340 --> 00:27:38,200
On the flip side, we would
expect the reverse. When

504
00:27:38,200 --> 00:27:41,110
we have a low electrolyte
concentration like

505
00:27:41,110 --> 00:27:46,160
hypokalemia and hypocalcemia,
we would expect a prolonged

506
00:27:46,160 --> 00:27:51,980
QT interval and a shorter
QRS complex duration.

507
00:27:52,400 --> 00:27:57,040
Again, this is a general
rule to keep in mind. It

508
00:27:57,040 --> 00:27:59,860
can help make it easier
to learn some of the ECG

509
00:27:59,860 --> 00:28:03,200
changes when it comes to
electrolyte disturbances. It

510
00:28:03,200 --> 00:28:06,960
works, again, about 90%
of the time. So keep this

511
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:11,300
in mind as you review, as
you go through ECGs with

512
00:28:11,640 --> 00:28:14,480
electrolyte anomalies.
It can make it easier

513
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:17,780
for you to kind of group
all the ECG changes and

514
00:28:17,780 --> 00:28:21,520
make it a little bit
easier to understand and

515
00:28:21,520 --> 00:28:25,240
have less to memorize
if we group changes in

516
00:28:25,240 --> 00:28:28,060
electrolytes based off
of groups and grouping.

517
00:28:32,300 --> 00:28:36,540
So as we mentioned, why
do we want to talk about

518
00:28:36,540 --> 00:28:38,440
electrolytes in general?
Well, when we have

519
00:28:38,440 --> 00:28:40,260
electrolyte disturbances,
we're going to worry about

520
00:28:40,260 --> 00:28:43,990
the heart. And potassium
is one of the big ions

521
00:28:44,160 --> 00:28:47,540
that we want to worry
about electrolytes. So with

522
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:50,460
potassium, as we mentioned,
going back to our

523
00:28:50,460 --> 00:28:52,760
general paradigm, what
we can expect to see when

524
00:28:52,760 --> 00:28:56,400
there's high potassium
is, on the ECG, is QT

525
00:28:56,400 --> 00:29:00,220
interval shortening and
winding of the QRS complex.

526
00:29:01,260 --> 00:29:04,380
One thing that we do want
to note that's pretty

527
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:09,040
specific for very high
potassium is tall, peak, narrow

528
00:29:09,040 --> 00:29:11,960
T waves. Now, one way to
think about this or remember

529
00:29:11,960 --> 00:29:14,320
this is to remember that
K, or potassium limbs

530
00:29:14,320 --> 00:29:18,110
lives under the T wave so
with hyperkalemia we get

531
00:29:18,110 --> 00:29:23,560
spiked or peaked T waves
and by peaked we mean T wave

532
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:29,180
amplitude that's bigger than
the QRS complex amplitude

533
00:29:29,180 --> 00:29:33,800
so this is a pretty high
yield ECG findings to

534
00:29:33,800 --> 00:29:37,400
know of especially because
it's specific to hyperkalemia

535
00:29:37,400 --> 00:29:40,280
so knowing this is going
to be important on exams

536
00:29:40,280 --> 00:29:42,440
and on boards and again
in clinical practice

537
00:29:42,440 --> 00:29:44,900
especially because this is
one of the first things you'll

538
00:29:44,900 --> 00:29:48,560
see with an elevated
potassium level, is the tall

539
00:29:48,740 --> 00:29:52,260
peaked T waves. As the
concentration continues to

540
00:29:52,260 --> 00:29:55,780
increase, then we see further
